# apps/cms (Jest 26.x / CRA-era @types) and apps/public (Jest 30.x / current
# @types) both depend, transitively, on several packages under the same name
# but incompatible major versions (jest and its whole @jest/* family,
# @types/react, @types/react-dom, @types/jest, expect, pretty-format,
# babel-*-jest). By default pnpm hoists
# every package (direct or transitive) into a single shared "compatibility"
# folder (node_modules/.pnpm/node_modules/<pkg>) so legacy tools that do a
# bare `require('pkg')` walk-up (like CRA/react-scripts, and apparently
# Next.js's own next/jest + TypeScript's automatic type-acquisition) can
# still find them without declaring them as real dependencies. When two
# incompatible versions of the same package exist in the workspace, pnpm's
# choice of which one lands in that shared folder is arbitrary/
# non-deterministic — and it picked the *wrong* (apps/cms, jest 26.x-era)
# version for several packages, which broke apps/public's jest/tsc runs
# with confusing "cannot read testEnvironmentOptions" and "ReactNode is not
# assignable to ReactNode" errors that had nothing to do with our own
# config.
#
# Excluding these families from the shared hoist forces every consumer to
# resolve them only through its own real (declared) dependency graph, which
# pnpm always gets right. apps/cms already declares jest/jest-environment-jsdom
# directly (see apps/cms/package.json) for the one thing CRA's own preflight
# check needs from this shared folder, so this exclusion does not change
# apps/cms's behavior — it only removes the ambiguous, wrongly-resolved
# entries that were breaking apps/public.
#
# IMPORTANT: the `!jest-*` glob below is broad enough to also match
# jest-watch-typeahead, a real transitive dependency of react-scripts/CRA's
# default jest config that has nothing to do with the jest-version collision
# this file exists to fix — excluding it from hoisting broke apps/cms's
# `test` script ("Watch plugin jest-watch-typeahead/filename cannot be
# found"). The explicit allow-back entry below restores normal hoisting for
# it.
#
# @types/react and @types/react-dom remain excluded on purpose. Excluding
# them breaks apps/cms's `build` (FileUpload.tsx's react-dropzone can't find
# an ambient @types/react to type its useDropzone() call against). However,
# allowing them to hoist trades that regression for a *different* one:
# apps/public's `build` then fails with a TS2344 "ReactNode is not
# assignable to ReactNode" error in its generated .next/types/validator.ts —
# the same class of duplicate-module-identity bug this file exists to
# prevent, just triggered from the other direction (Next's internal 'react'
# type resolution and apps/public's own resolution end up pointing at two
# logically-distinct instances of the identical @types/react version once
# it's hoisted into the shared store). This was verified directly: hoisting
# @types/react (not @types/react-dom, which is not the trigger) reproducibly
# breaks apps/public's build in a clean install, independent of Node version.
#
# Fixing apps/cms's build without this trade requires either a code-level
# fix in FileUpload.tsx (and likely ImageUpload.tsx/react-image-crop,
# QRCode.tsx/react-qr-code, and other apps/cms UI deps that hit the same
# ambient-@types/react-not-found issue once excluded — this is not a single
# narrow fix) or per-package pnpm.packageExtensions entries enumerated for
# every affected dependency. Do not "fix" apps/cms's build by just removing
# this exclusion — it silently breaks apps/public instead.
#
# UPDATE: the FileUpload.tsx build failure was root-caused and fixed via
# root package.json's `pnpm.packageExtensions` (NOT here). The mechanism:
# packages like react-dropzone/react-image-crop/react-qr-code/react-dnd/
# react-datetime/react-final-form(-arrays)/react-i18next/react-markdown
# ship their own bundled .d.ts (not a separate @types/<pkg> package) and
# internally write `import * as React from 'react'` without declaring
# `@types/react` as a peerDependency. With `@types/react` excluded from
# hoisting above, TypeScript's module resolution walk from these packages'
# location inside the pnpm virtual store (node_modules/.pnpm/<pkg>@ver/...)
# can no longer find ANY @types/react — not apps/cms's own (it's a sibling
# subtree, not an ancestor directory of the store package) and not the
# shared hoist folder (excluded on purpose, see above). Confirmed via
# `tsc --traceResolution`: resolution for 'react' from react-dropzone's
# own .d.ts fell through to the untyped runtime `react/index.js`, so
# `React.HTMLProps<HTMLElement>` inside that .d.ts resolved to `any`, and
# `Pick<any, "multiple" | "onDragEnter" | ...>` produced REQUIRED `any`
# properties (mapped-type semantics) instead of the real optional ones —
# hence "missing properties onDragEnter, onDragLeave, onDragOver, multiple"
# in FileUpload.tsx, not an actual type mismatch. skipLibCheck (already
# true in both tsconfigs) does not help: the error surfaces in FileUpload.tsx
# (app code), not inside the .d.ts itself.
#
# The fix declares `@types/react` as an explicit peerDependency of just
# those packages via `pnpm.packageExtensions`, which makes pnpm resolve a
# dedicated, peer-scoped copy of each package (e.g.
# react-dropzone@14.4.1_@types+react@16.8.14_react@16.14.0) that reaches
# its own @types/react through normal declared-dependency resolution —
# completely independent of the shared hoist folder and its exclusions.
# This required enumerating apps/cms's react-* dependencies once (a fixed,
# bounded list, checked via each package's own `types`/`typings` field
# rather than iteratively chasing whack-a-mole build failures), not an
# unbounded ongoing list. See root package.json.
hoist-pattern[]=*
hoist-pattern[]=!jest
hoist-pattern[]=!jest-*
hoist-pattern[]=jest-watch-typeahead
hoist-pattern[]=!@jest/*
hoist-pattern[]=!babel-jest
hoist-pattern[]=!babel-preset-jest
hoist-pattern[]=!babel-plugin-jest-hoist
hoist-pattern[]=!expect
hoist-pattern[]=!pretty-format
hoist-pattern[]=!@types/react
hoist-pattern[]=!@types/react-dom
hoist-pattern[]=!@types/jest
